[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:One Man's Meat is Another Man's Poison]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.35]Lesson 23 | 第23课
[00:02.28]One man's meat is another man's poison | 各有所爱
[00:11.55]What was it about snails that made the writer collect them for his friend on that day in particular? | 究竟是什么让作者在那一天特意为朋友收集蜗牛？
[00:20.29]People become quite illogical when they try to decide what can be eaten and what cannot be eaten. | 在决定什么能吃、什么不能吃的时候，人们往往变得毫无逻辑。
[00:28.51]If you lived in the Mediterranean, for instance, you would consider octopus a great delicacy. | 比如，如果你住在地中海地区，你会把章鱼视为美味佳肴。
[00:35.92]You would not be able to understand why some people find it repulsive. | 你就无法理解为什么有人会觉得它倒胃口。
[00:41.78]On the other hand, your stomach would turn at the idea of frying potatoes in animal fat-the normally accepted practice in many northern countries. | 反过来，一想到用动物油炸土豆，你便会感到恶心——而这在许多北方国家却是普遍接受的习俗。
[00:53.91]The sad truth is that most of us have been brought up to eat certain foods and we stick to them all our lives. | 令人遗憾的是，我们中的大多数人都是在吃某些食物的环境中长大的，并终生坚持吃这些食物。
[01:03.19]No creature has received more praise and abuse than the common garden snail. | 没有什么生物受到的赞誉和辱骂会比普通花园里的蜗牛更多。
[01:09.56]Cooked in wine, snails are a great luxury in various parts of the world. | 用酒烹调的蜗牛在世界各地都是一种奢侈品。
[01:15.43]There are countless people who, ever since their early years, have learned to associate snails with food. | 无数的人从幼年起就知道把蜗牛和美味联系在一起。
[01:24.20]My friend, Robert, lives in a country where snails are despised. | 我的朋友罗伯特生活在一个厌恶蜗牛的国家。
[01:30.31]As his flat is in a large town, he has no garden of his own. | 由于他的公寓在大城市里，他没有自己的花园。
[01:35.31]For years he has been asking me to collect snails from my garden and take them to him. | 多年来，他一直让我从我的花园里为他收集蜗牛。
[01:42.46]The idea never appealed to me very much, but one day, after a heavy shower, | 这个主意从来不怎么吸引我，但有一天，一场大雨过后，
[01:49.17]I happened to be walking in my garden when I noticed a huge number of snails taking a stroll on some of my prize plants. | 我碰巧在花园里散步，突然注意到大量蜗牛正在我的一些珍贵植物上漫步。
[01:58.55]Acting on a sudden impulse, I collected several dozen, put them in a paper bag, and took them to Robert. | 我一时冲动，捉了几十只，装进一只纸袋，带给了罗伯特。
[02:07.01]Robert was delighted to see me and equally pleased with my little gift. | 罗伯特见到我很高兴，对我的小礼物也同样满意。
[02:13.03]I left the bag in the hall and Robert and I went into the living room where we talked for a couple of hours. | 我把口袋放在门厅里，和罗伯特一起进了客厅，聊了几个小时。
[02:20.56]I had forgotten all about the snails when Robert suddenly said that I must stay to dinner. | 我已把蜗牛的事忘得一干二净，这时罗伯特突然提出我必须留下来吃饭。
[02:27.73]Snails would, of course, be the main dish. | 蜗牛当然会是主菜。
[02:31.79]I did not fancy the idea and I reluctantly followed Robert out of the room. | 我可不想吃蜗牛，但又不情愿地跟着罗伯特走出了房间。
[02:37.90]To our dismay, | 使我们惊愕的是，
[02:39.70]we saw that there were snails everywhere: | 我们看到到处都是蜗牛：
[02:42.97]they had escaped from the paper bag | 它们从纸袋里逃了出来，
[02:45.40]and had taken complete possession of the hall! I have never be able to look at a snail since then. | 完全占领了门厅！从那以后，我再也无法正眼看蜗牛了。
